# Batch files are read by cmd.exe, which resumes a running script by BYTE OFFSET
# after each command. update.bat pulls a new copy of itself mid-run, so its
# resume pad is sized in bytes for CRLF. A contributor with core.autocrlf=input
# converting these to LF would shift every offset and land execution somewhere
# other than the pad. Pin the line endings rather than relying on nobody doing it.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Runs inside the Linux container, built from whatever checkout the user has.
# A CRLF copy (core.autocrlf=true on Windows) would break the #!/bin/sh line
# and the container would not start.
scripts/docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# The pre-release (develop 663523c) update.bat, kept byte for byte (CRLF) for the
# README-recovery scenarios: no end-of-line conversion on any platform.
scripts/fixtures/update.bat.develop-663523c -text

# Runs inside the Linux container, mounted from the checkout by restore.sh AND
# restore.bat (the rollback after a failed full restore). A CRLF copy on
# Windows would not run under the container's /bin/sh.
scripts/snapshot-restore.sh text eol=lf
